# Forced-Labour Exploitation State Machine Extension (T-Box + phase/action vocabulary)
#
# Narrow, machine-only extension: contributes ONLY what the trajectories
# (traj:) Exploitation State Machine needs to run on a forced-labour /
# labour-trafficking offense — a skos:ConceptScheme of traj:State phases
# and a small catalog of uco-action:Technique-typed action classes. No
# domain taxonomy beyond the UCO 1.5.0 uco-action:Technique metaclass
# punning pattern. These are offense-conduct techniques, not MITRE ATT&CK
# hacking techniques; the attack-technique catalog is not a dependency.
#
# The fl: alphabet predates this file. extensions/layered has occupied
# fl:Recruitment, fl:Control and fl:ForcedLabor and typed actions with
# fl:a_coerce (renamed fl:a_coerce_labor here, so the registry local name
# does not collide with trafficking:a_coerce) and fl:a_compel_labor since
# layered 0.2.0, declaring the
# states inline in its Atkinson exemplar because no forced-labor package
# existed. This extension declares that alphabet once, keeps every IRI the
# layered exemplar already uses, and adds the two phases and two techniques
# a complete labour-exploitation chain needs.
#
# CAC crosswalk policy (same as extensions/layered). No CAC class is
# retyped and CAC is never owl:imported. CAC has no forced-labour module,
# and its trafficking module (cacontology-sex-trafficking.ttl, CAC 3.1.0)
# is scoped to sex trafficking of children, so skos:exactMatch is not
# used. Two phases overlap classes that module declares and carry a
# documentation-only skos:closeMatch:
#   fl:Recruitment -> cacontology-trafficking:VictimRecruitment
#   fl:Control     -> cacontology-trafficking:VictimControlSituation
# fl:Control is a state, so it is matched to the Situation class rather
# than to the VictimControl event. ForcedLabor and WageAppropriation have
# no CAC counterpart.
#
# Alphabet grounding. The states follow the ILO definition of forced
# labour (Convention No. 29, 1930: work "exacted from any person under the
# menace of any penalty and for which the said person has not offered
# himself voluntarily") and the ILO's eleven indicators of forced labour
# (ILO/SAP-FL, 2012; revised edition 2025): abuse of vulnerability,
# deception, restriction of movement, isolation, physical and sexual
# violence, intimidation and threats, retention of identity documents,
# withholding of wages, debt bondage, abusive working and living
# conditions, excessive overtime. Forced labour is defined by Convention
# No. 29 and exists with or without trafficking. The UN Trafficking in
# Persons Protocol (Palermo, 2000, art. 3(a)) applies when a chain also
# has a trafficking act: it defines trafficking as act (recruitment,
# transportation, transfer, harbouring, receipt), means (threat or use of
# force, coercion, fraud, deception, abuse of a position of vulnerability)
# and purpose, and lists "forced labour or services" among the purposes.
# A chain that includes a recruitment-type act, carried out by a listed
# means, for the purpose of forced labour, falls within that definition.
# Occupying fl:Recruitment establishes none of those three on its own: the
# act is the enacted action, the means is what its instrument records, and
# the purpose is the terminal state the chain reaches. A chain that starts
# at Control with no recruitment act in the source (the Cyprus case) is
# forced labour under Convention No. 29 and makes no trafficking claim.
#
#   Recruitment        -- Palermo act (recruitment); ILO indicator
#                          "deception" when the promise is false.
#   Control            -- ILO indicators "retention of identity documents",
#                          "intimidation and threats", "restriction of
#                          movement", "isolation", "abuse of vulnerability",
#                          "debt bondage"; Palermo means.
#   ForcedLabor        -- ILO definition (work exacted under menace of a
#                          penalty); indicators "abusive working and living
#                          conditions", "excessive overtime".
#   WageAppropriation  -- ILO indicator "withholding of wages"; Palermo
#                          purpose ("forced labour or services").
#
# Instances grounded in the Europol newsroom release "27 arrests and 248
# potential victims identified in action week against labour exploitation"
# (2026-10-23, Europol and the European Labour Authority; action week
# 14-20 September 2026). Two highlighted cases supply the exemplar; no
# suspect or victim is named in the source and none is named here.
#
#   Recruitment        -- Romania: workers, some of them minors, taken
#                          on for farm work under a false promise of
#                          fair pay.
#   Control            -- Cyprus: the employer kept the worker's
#                          passport, so he could not leave or go to the
#                          authorities.
#   ForcedLabor        -- Cyprus: ten-hour days with no rest days;
#                          Romania: working days over twelve hours.
#   WageAppropriation  -- Cyprus and Romania: no wages paid.
#
# A press release is thin source material: no phase, action, amount or
# affordance is asserted beyond what the release states.

@prefix fl: <http://example.org/ontology/forced-labor/> .
@prefix traj: <http://example.org/ontology/trajectories/> .
@prefix uco-core: <https://ontology.unifiedcyberontology.org/uco/core/> .
@prefix uco-action: <https://ontology.unifiedcyberontology.org/uco/action/> .
@prefix cacontology-trafficking: <https://cacontology.projectvic.org/trafficking#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/ontology/forced-labor>
	a owl:Ontology ;
	rdfs:label "Forced-Labour Exploitation State Machine Extension"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Phase vocabulary (traj:State plug-in) and Technique-typed action catalog for a forced-labour / labour-trafficking Exploitation State Machine. Declares the fl: alphabet that the layered extension's Atkinson exemplar occupies. Grounded in the Europol/ELA labour-exploitation action week of 14-20 September 2026. Depends on the trajectories extension and the UCO 1.5.0 uco-action:Technique metaclass; mints no domain taxonomy beyond phases and actions; no CAC class is retyped or imported; Recruitment and Control carry a skos:closeMatch to CAC 3.1.0 trafficking classes."@en ;
	dcterms:source
		"https://www.ilo.org/publications/ilo-indicators-forced-labour"^^xsd:anyURI ,
		"https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-mechanisms/instruments/protocol-prevent-suppress-and-punish-trafficking-persons"^^xsd:anyURI ,
		"https://www.europol.europa.eu/media-press/newsroom/news/27-arrests-and-248-potential-victims-identified-in-action-week-against-labour-exploitation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	owl:imports
		<http://example.org/ontology/trajectories> ,
		<https://ontology.unifiedcyberontology.org/uco/action> .

#################################################################
# Phase vocabulary: traj:State instances in a SKOS scheme
#################################################################

fl:PhaseScheme
	a skos:ConceptScheme ;
	skos:prefLabel "Forced-labour offense phases"@en ;
	dcterms:source "Europol newsroom, 27 arrests and 248 potential victims identified in action week against labour exploitation, 2026-10-23"@en .

fl:Recruitment
	a traj:State , skos:Concept ;
	uco-core:name "Recruitment" ;
	skos:prefLabel "Recruitment"@en ;
	skos:definition "The worker is engaged for the labour, often on a false basis. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Romania ('deceived into agricultural work with promises of fair pay')."@en ;
	skos:closeMatch cacontology-trafficking:VictimRecruitment ;
	skos:inScheme fl:PhaseScheme .

fl:Control
	a traj:State , skos:Concept ;
	uco-core:name "Control" ;
	skos:prefLabel "Control"@en ;
	skos:definition "The worker's freedom to leave, complain or seek help is restricted: identity documents withheld, threats, housing or food dependency, irregular immigration status. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Cyprus ('withheld the victim's passport'), Netherlands ('used threats'), Poland ('exploiting their irregular immigration status to prevent them from reporting abuse')."@en ;
	skos:closeMatch cacontology-trafficking:VictimControlSituation ;
	skos:inScheme fl:PhaseScheme .

fl:ForcedLabor
	a traj:State , skos:Concept ;
	uco-core:name "Forced labor" ;
	skos:prefLabel "Forced labor"@en ;
	skos:definition "The worker performs labour under hours and conditions they cannot refuse. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Cyprus ('10-hour days with no pay or days off'), Romania ('over 12 hours working days')."@en ;
	skos:inScheme fl:PhaseScheme .

fl:WageAppropriation
	a traj:State , skos:Concept ;
	uco-core:name "Wage appropriation" ;
	skos:prefLabel "Wage appropriation"@en ;
	skos:definition "Wages for the labour performed are withheld or underpaid and the value is retained by the offender. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Cyprus and Romania ('no pay'), Netherlands ('underpaid them ... to generate at least EUR 4 million in profits')."@en ;
	skos:inScheme fl:PhaseScheme .

#################################################################
# Action catalog: Technique-typed uco-action:Action subclasses
# (UCO 1.5.0 uco-action:Technique metaclass punning pattern)
#################################################################

fl:a_deceive_recruit
	a owl:Class , uco-action:Technique ;
	rdfs:subClassOf uco-action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Recruit by deception"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Engaging a worker with a false promise about pay or conditions. Affordance abused: the job offer. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Romania ('deceived ... with promises of fair pay')."@en ;
	uco-action:techniqueID "FL.T1" .

fl:a_coerce_labor
	a owl:Class , uco-action:Technique ;
	rdfs:subClassOf uco-action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Coerce continued labour"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Compelling a worker's continued labour by document retention, threats, dependency or immigration status. Affordance abused: the withheld document, the threat, or the dependency. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Cyprus and Netherlands."@en ;
	uco-action:techniqueID "FL.T2" .

fl:a_compel_labor
	a owl:Class , uco-action:Technique ;
	rdfs:subClassOf uco-action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Compel labour"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Imposing working hours and conditions the worker cannot refuse. Affordance abused: the job placement. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Cyprus and Romania."@en ;
	uco-action:techniqueID "FL.T3" .

fl:a_appropriate_wages
	a owl:Class , uco-action:Technique ;
	rdfs:subClassOf uco-action:Action ;
	rdfs:label "Appropriate wages"@en ;
	rdfs:comment "Withholding or underpaying wages for the labour performed and retaining the value. Affordance abused: control of the payroll. Source: Europol 2026-10-23, Cyprus, Romania and Netherlands."@en ;
	uco-action:techniqueID "FL.T4" .
